#section("IV. A Compact Framing")

If the resemblance and the rupture are to be held in one thought, it is this. Kant
gives us an _epistemically modest, asymptotic_ version of eternal progression:
endless approach, never arrival, with no claim to know it is true — a trajectory
reason must posit for the sake of morality, terminating in a God it may not
describe. The Latter-day Saint tradition gives us an _epistemically confident,
teleological_ version: actual arrival, doctrinally asserted, with ontological
continuity between the human and the divine — a trajectory revelation discloses,
terminating in a God the traveler becomes.

The family resemblance is genuine, and it is not superficial. Both refuse a single,
instantaneous, static salvation-event; both make the self the kind of thing that is
not wholly constituted from outside; both make perfection a matter of process rather
than of sudden bestowal. A comparison that denied the resemblance would be as false
as one that overstated it. But the two diverge sharply, and along the axes that
matter most to each: on _how confidently_ one is permitted to say that perfection is
a process — a permission Kant restricts to moral faith and the tradition extends to
revealed knowledge — and on _whether the gap_ between creature and God ever actually
closes, which Kant fixes open as the condition of moral striving and the tradition
declares closed as the promise of exaltation.

What the comparison finally exposes is a trade each tradition makes. Kant purchases
epistemic honesty at the cost of arrival: the price of never overclaiming about the
supersensible is that the creature never crosses the gap, and holiness remains
forever a standard rather than an attainment @kant-cpracr[Ak. 5:122]. The tradition
purchases arrival at the cost of epistemic modesty: the price of promising real
deification is a confidence about premortal intelligence, embodied deity, and the
structure of the afterlife that Kant's whole critical apparatus was built to forbid
@king-follett. Neither trade is obviously the wrong one. But they are different
trades, and seeing that they are different — that the shared refusal of static
salvation branches, almost immediately, into two incompatible economies of hope —
is what the comparison is for.
